+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+The record store accepts visit submissions from tablets that may remain offline for days. Two workers can independently correct the same visit after comparing field notes, so a visit can have several submitted versions derived from the same starting state. The store must define what the visit's current state means, how competing submissions become review work, and which visits can enter the partner CSV. The approach determines the submission metadata tablet-sync must retain, the shape of review-export's comparison work, and the point at which a visit becomes export-eligible.
+
+A visit's scientific fields are observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Others are scientifically related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The record store cannot infer every such relationship from field names alone.
+
+Every option must preserve the submitted payloads and keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable. Last-write-wins does not meet that constraint because it discards a submitted edit; it is not an option in contention.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Whole-visit scientific integrity** — major. Does the mechanism ever put a current record into use that no worker submitted, by combining fields across independently edited copies? This criterion carries the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*: preserving a payload is not enough if the exported record is a synthesis whose scientific meaning no worker reviewed as a whole.
+- **Coordinator review workload** — major. Two coordinators absorb the season's review work. An approach that creates review work for every concurrent edit must remain operable at that staffing level; one that leaves more visits ready for export reduces the queue.
+- **Explainability of the exported state** — major. A coordinator or the science partner must be able to see which submission produced the exported values and why competing submissions were set aside. This criterion carries the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Maintenance burden** — moderate. Rules that combine fields must be specified, tested, and kept correct as visit structure changes. The team has no dedicated systems administrator, so standing rule maintenance competes directly with coordinator time.
+- **Offline submission and local storage burden** — moderate. Tablets can be disconnected for 48 hours and must retain enough state to submit an edit days after fetching its starting state. This criterion carries the tenet *Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*.
+- **Export readiness** — moderate. Visits with unresolved review work are held out of the Friday CSV. An approach that resolves more edits automatically gets more visits into the export, but throughput does not override scientific integrity.
+- **Ability to reconsider the choice later** — minor. The team's edit patterns are not yet measured, so an approach that can be revisited without rewriting stored history is preferable.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+**Description.** Every submission is a complete visit payload derived from a named base revision. The store keeps an immutable revision tree per visit, a pointer to the current revision, and the branch heads still awaiting resolution. A revision derived directly from the current revision advances the current revision. A revision derived from any other revision becomes a pending revision. A coordinator resolves competing revisions by choosing one complete revision; the store records the choice, the coordinator, and the revisions that contributed to the comparison. The store never combines fields across revisions.
+
+**Demonstration.** A visit's current revision is `R1`. Worker A submits `R2`, a complete visit with a corrected observer name, naming `R1` as its base. Worker B submits `R3`, a complete visit with a missing species added, also naming `R1` as its base. `R2` becomes current; `R3` is pending. Review-export shows `R2` and `R3` as two complete competing visits. The coordinator chooses one, and that revision's payload becomes the exported state.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Whole-visit scientific integrity: **strong**. Every candidate is exactly the visit a worker submitted. No current record is assembled from fields of different submissions.
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak**. Every concurrent edit creates review work, even when the edits touch unrelated fields.
+- Explainability of the exported state: **strong**. The exported payload is a retained submission, and the resolution record names the coordinator, the chosen revision, and the revisions compared.
+- Maintenance burden: **strong**. There are no field-merge rules to maintain; the logic is revision lineage, pending branch heads, and resolution.
+- Offline submission and local storage burden: **strong**. Tablets retain a base revision ID and submit a complete visit; they do not generate or store structured field changes.
+- Export readiness: **weak**. Any visit with a pending revision is held out of the CSV until a coordinator resolves it.
+- Ability to reconsider the choice later: **strong**. All original revisions remain stored, so a later policy can read the same history without rewriting submissions.
+
+**Summary.** The approach accepts a larger review queue and lower immediate export readiness to guarantee that the exported visit is one a worker actually submitted.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+**Description.** Every submission carries the base state the worker started from plus structured changes to fields. The store automatically combines changes whose fields are disjoint. Changes that touch the same field require coordinator review. The store maintains combination rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and retains the original submissions alongside the combined result. Review-export would need field-level provenance so a coordinator can see which submission supplied each field.
+
+**Demonstration.** A visit's base records observer "A. Ng" and notes "erosion at north edge." Worker A changes the notes; worker B adds a species. Because the changed fields are disjoint, the store combines both changes into one current visit without review. If both workers change the cover estimate for the same species, the store marks that species entry as overlapping and sends the visit to review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Whole-visit scientific integrity: **weak**. A combined record can cross relationships the field rules do not encode. A species identification change and a cover estimate change may occupy different fields yet be scientifically dependent; automatic combination can produce a visit no worker reviewed as a whole.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong**. Disjoint edits combine automatically, so unrelated corrections do not consume coordinator time.
+- Explainability of the exported state: **partial**. Field-level provenance can show which submission supplied each value, but the combined visit remains a synthesis that no worker submitted; explaining the exported record requires both provenance and the merge rules that produced it.
+- Maintenance burden: **weak**. Rules for scalar replacement, observer-list membership, and species-entry identity must be specified, tested, and maintained, and their behavior must stay legible to coordinators.
+- Offline submission and local storage burden: **partial**. Tablets retain base snapshots and compute structured changes, increasing local state and submission machinery compared with sending a complete visit.
+- Export readiness: **strong**. Visits whose edits are disjoint become export-eligible without coordinator work.
+- Ability to reconsider the choice later: **partial**. Original submissions are retained, but a later whole-visit policy would need to reconstruct candidate visits from stored changes.
+
+**Summary.** The approach trades maintained merge semantics and synthesized current records for less review work and more visits ready for export.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions are selected. The decision turns on whole-visit scientific integrity and explainability, both major criteria, and is reinforced by the lower maintenance and offline burdens. Field-aware merging is stronger on review workload and export readiness, but its advantage is purchased by combining fields according to rules that do not understand every scientific relationship. Two coordinators can review whole competing visits without having to trust and maintain a merge rule set, and an exported value can always be traced to a submitted visit and a recorded resolution. This follows the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and it fits a workflow two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator.
+
+The team accepts the larger review queue as the cost of that guarantee. The choice is a trade, not a finding that field-aware merging is wrong: it would be reconsidered if measured concurrent-edit volume overwhelms two coordinators, if disjoint edits dominate and the queue becomes mostly routine, or if the team adopts an explicit merge rule set with field-level provenance that coordinators can audit. Those conditions would change the balance of the two major workload and integrity criteria; they would not change the requirement that every submitted payload remain recoverable.
+
+**Obligations this creates downstream.**
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain each draft's base revision ID, submit complete visits with that base, and display pending competing revisions and the visit's unresolved status after sync. It does not need to compute field diffs or maintain merge rules, but it must handle a visit that becomes unresolved because another worker submitted from the same base.
+- **Review-export** must present complete competing revisions for comparison, record a coordinator's chosen revision through the resolution operation, and hold unresolved visits out of the partner CSV. It does not need field-level provenance or merge rules, but its comparison view must show whole visits with their authors and receipt times, and its export path must show the resolution that selected the exported revision.
+- **Record-store** must store the revision tree, the pending branch heads, the current revision pointer, and immutable resolution records, and must reject a resolution whose reviewed pending set has changed.
+
+**What this forecloses.** Disjoint field edits no longer combine automatically. A visit with any pending revision waits for a coordinator even when the competing edits are scientifically unrelated.
+
+**What this leaves open.** Whether a coordinator may submit a corrected revision after resolving a visit through the ordinary submission operation, and whether the store rejects a new visit against an archived plot. Both are recorded as deferred decisions in the record store design.
